===Etymology===
[[File:Table of Trigonometry, Cyclopaedia, Volume 2.jpg|thumb|Gunter’s lines are visible on this drawing of a [[Gunter's scale|Gunter’s scale]] ''(right, marked “Fig. 35”)''.{{ref|group=n|name=n1|From {{cite-book|author=E[phraim] Chambers|authorlink=Ephraim Chambers|title=[[w:Cyclopædia, or an Universal Dictionary of Arts and Sciences|Cyclopædia: Or, An Universal Dictionary of Arts and Sciences;{{nb...|Containing the Definitions of the Terms, and Accounts of the Things Signify’d thereby, in the Several Arts, both Liberal and Mechanical, and the Several Sciences, Human and Divine: [...] In Two Volumes.}}]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed for}} James and John Knapton [''et al.'']|year=1728|volume=II|section=plate between pages 252 and 253|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/gri_33125011134307/page/n906/mode/1up|oclc=951657352}}.}}]]

From {{affix|en|Gunter|-'s|line}}, from the surname of the English [[clergyman]], [[geometer]], and [[mathematician]] {{w|Edmund Gunter}} (1581–1628), the inventor of the [[Gunter's scale|Gunter’s scale]].<ref>{{R:OED Online|subentry=Gunter's line|subid=2356956|entry=Gunter|pos=n|id=82604|date=June 2019}}</ref>

